AI Skill Configuration: The Gospel-Centered Sermon Framework

1. Executive Philosophy: The "A to Z" Gospel Thesis

The homiletical imperative for the modern preacher is a strategic shift from viewing the gospel as a "minimal entry requirement" (the ABCs) to the "comprehensive operating system" (the A to Z) for all of life. Too often, sermons treat the gospel as the rudimentary gate through which one enters the kingdom, only to leave it behind in favor of "biblical principles" and moral effort for the remainder of the Christian life. This is a catastrophic architectural error. The gospel is not merely the way into the kingdom; it is the way we solve every problem, grow through every difficulty, and achieve concrete character transformation.

To operationalize this, the preacher must understand the "Worldview Grid." A worldview is not a set of beliefs we look at, but a set of foundational convictions we look through to interpret reality. Unless a sermon explicitly provides a new gospel grid, the listener will automatically process the message through their default "Moralistic Grid." Without this architectural intervention, the listener hears "Good News" as "Good Advice" and processes an invitation to grace as a demand for better performance.

Gospel Grid vs. Traditional Grids

Category Moralistic Grid Relativistic Grid Gospel Grid
Source of Identity Moral record, performance, and achievement. Cultural constructions or radical self-determination. Received status through the imputed righteousness of Christ.
Motivation for Change Fear of rejection; the need to earn or maintain blessing. Lack of objective moral imperative; subjective preference. Gratitude and love-based fellowship with the Father.
